hello i'm sanskrita  and i'm very interested in understanding  what makes or breaks or immune systems  cancer is one such question and hence my  topic  fun fact in the background of the slide  is an image of  pd1 expression and t cell surface as  seen by fluorescence microscope  we will discuss a little bit about the  pd1 receptors in the upcoming slides  so moving on  in the duration of the next few minutes  i will be speaking about immunotherapy  specifically immune checkpoint  inhibitors in the context of non-small  cell lung cancer and also discuss the  results of analysis of gene expression  during therapy  so first about non-small cell lung  cancer  or nsclc when we look at the diagnostic  and  mortality figures of cancer lung cancer  trumps all other types causing almost 20  percent of the deaths and non-small cell  lung cancer accounts for 90 percent of  these deaths  therefore a rigorous study of nsclc and  our therapeutic options are very  important  currently the doctors prefer surgical  resection and radiotherapy for early  stage patients  while chemotherapy is provided to late  stage patients  our last confirmed data packs nsclc  debts to a close  to a close 1.62 million clearly we need  improved therapeutic modalities  so immunotherapy  immunotherapy manipulates  the immune system's own effectors to  deal with these disease conditions i'm  going to focus more on the immune  checkpoint inhibitors here  so the receptors program death 1 or pd1  and cytotoxic t-cell associated protein  or ctla4 are expressed by almost all  types of t cells  dynamically across time and magnitude of  immune response  so in normal conditions they would  interact with their ligands on dendritic  cells and suppress the immune response  this forms the basis of autoimmunity  prevention which is essential but cancer  cells taking advantage of it also  express uh ligands pdl1 and b7 which can  help them to mute the immune response  when it is actually required  the interaction between pd1 and its  ligand sets off a cascade  it causes effector t cell dysfunction  exhaustion and neutralization of  maturity cells prevents further  activation of naive t cells and also can  activate the regulatory diesels  all these together produce a cytokine  brew that makes the tumor micro  environment highly inflamed  the tumor growth becomes more aggressive  and invasive gradually shutting down all  branches of immune system  so immune checkpoint inhibitors  interfere in the first step of this  process blocking any interaction between  the receptors and their ligands by  competitive binding to one or the other  even in the league of tumors nsclc shows  extremely high mutational burden so it  becomes very affable to this form of  intervention  the data that i have analyzed is tri  trial data of checkpoint inhibitor  nivolumab  it is a humanized antibody that binds  specifically to the receptor pd1  although it is theoretically a very  robust therapy many failures have been  observed in the form of adverse immune  events and or progressive disease  so the two questions i had going into  the study was whether genes expressed in  cytotoxic t cells  have any influence over the response  towards nivolumab therapy and if  administration of nivolumab influences  the gene expression in these cells so i  got the data set from jio and here is  the summary of the available information  so most of the patients had already  tried either surgery radiation or chemo  and did not see any improvement  so they were put on a course of  nivolumab and their serum samples  containing uh peripheral blood  mononuclear cells or lymphocytes  extracted before administration and six  to eight weeks after the administration  so before we move further a small point  needs clarity so response here refers  only to partial response throughout the  study in case of lung cancer  we generally can't hope for anything  better than that  now we can talk about the steps taken to  answer the questions i had  so the sequence reads uh were found to  be multiplexed and they had to be  demultiplexed before alignment and  mapping  boutique and are some expression table  algorithms on the t by info platform  were chosen for alignment mapping and  generating read counts  so  once the expression data was obtained in  expected counts genes that had low  average counts were eliminated before  performing further analysis  then based on the questions i wanted to  answer i created different groups of  data for exploratory analysis and  differential expression analysis  so on to the results of these analysis  starting with pca for exploratory  analysis  the first graph seen here is uh  for a general analysis of how the data  segregates based on expression values  this file contained information  regarding responders and non-responders  labeled at time points before and after  the administration of nivolumab  so the only  slightly clearer clustering is seen in  the violet dots on this side of the  graph  with one outlier here  and  these are from non-responders at the  final time point  this does not seem to be very useful  for us  and an outlier excluded pc analysis  could be performed but this data is  spared so excluding this outlier would  mean that we would have to explode  exclude some green point as well  this is a small patient group and this  does not seem to be a very optimal  solution  so i  went ahead and tried to analyze the  expression values as a whole at the  initial time points and final time  points and between responders and  non-responders with  no clear distinction again  to see if a new lumab influences  expression in killer t cells a large pc  analysis was performed for just the  response responders between initial and  final time point even this does not  provide any insights into the  relationship between expression values  and the known phenotypes however if we  look at the first uh three pca plots  this one and the first two in this page  we see that there is a very distinct  separation between the data points  right down the middle for reference  so this clustering is not explained by  any of the groups or definitions  provided by the data this points to some  missing sample information that could  help us understand the interaction  between nevolumab and cd8 positive t  cells better  but it's not available at the moment  now um on to the differential gene  expression analysis so the first  analysis was performed uh  between  responders and non-responders at time  point n1 to understand if gene  expression could somehow influence the  therapeutic response  here is a volcano plot  to represent the down regulation and up  regulation in genes with their  significance so to the left of the graph  are the down regulated genes and to the  right are the upregulated genes  lower negative  log p values indicate  low significance  and as we can see most of the genes have  a negative log p adjusted value of below  one  and the actual p adjusted value is very  high and upon a preliminary sorting of  the output table the smallest p adjusted  value came out to be 0.13 which is much  higher than the  preferred 0.5 threshold  so this finalizes the fact that  differential expression in responders  and non-responders before administration  of new lumab is  not significant enough and hence cannot  possibly influence the therapeutic  response  then  the second group of analysis  were the expression  in killer t-cells of respondents between  the initial and final time points we are  looking to see if nivolumab influences  the expression in its course of action  so one glance at the volcano plot shows  us that it does not by a lot but it does  only three significantly down regulated  genes were observed and these results  prevent us from moving ahead to analyze  pathways and gene ontology terms that  are differentially expressed  but  these three genes are also involved  heavily with many important cancer cell  deaths and immune response pathways  all of these genes  produce  long known non-coding rna and are  extremely essential in the regulation of  protein coding pathways although they  are unlikely to be translated themselves  so block one is transcript is uh shown  to be involved in two important pathways  and its down regulation is associated  with  silenced t cell factor four  t cell factor four uh proteins along  with other proteins of the tcf lef  family  are directly involved in inducing the  transcription of genes downstream of the  wnt pathway so the expression of these  genes influences t cell development and  differentiation at various time points  and tissue types like thymus lymph nodes  and tumor micro environment  these gene products can act in both  stimulatory and inhibitory ways  and a decrease in stimulation of wnt  signaling pathway improves naive cd8  positive t cell conversion into mature  cells and reduces fox p3 positive uh  transcriptional activity in  regulatory t cells  hence the down regulation of block 1s as  seen by the graph can deal with  effective t cell exhaustion and improve  t cell activation and reduce immune  suppression in the tumor micro  environment  so  tc4c silencing also directly improves  anti-tumor activity of  antigen presenting cells and thus can  help in delaying cancer growth  block 1s is also associated with  ferrotosis which is different from  apoptosis and necrosis it is a novel  cell death pathway and we won't be going  into the details regarding that however  proposals have been made to use genes  associated with ferrotosis as diagnostic  and prognostic markers in breast cancer  and a few other tumor types  so studies for its use in lung cancer  prognosis have not been conducted yet  but this could be a good starting point  and then coming to the next gene the  d7 uh tcam 2  could be  the whole locus near this gene is  associated with the tram trif pathway  a down regulation in the tme d70 cam 2  could be associated with reduced  inflammation  in tumor micro environment and this  could reduce the number of regulatory t  cells near tumors and further prevent  the conversion of helper t cells into  regulatory t cells for a bit of  clarification although the study  analyzes expression in cytotoxic t-cells  of adaptive system the responses of  adaptive and the innate system are  usually influenced by cells from either  of the systems in our body  so  yeah  anyway about the third gene  not a lot of information is available  about available about it for now  hopefully we'll be able to study how it  factors into all of this soon  now here's a recap of the questions  before we summarize the results  the pca plots show a distinct separation  undefined by available data there seems  to be some confounding variable  influencing the separation or based on  gene expression  and based on differential analysis gene  expression in tumor infiltrating  cytotoxic t cells cannot influence  response to nepolumab and cannot be used  as a marker for predicting the response  either  however  nivolumab does affect  gene expression in these cells and can  be used as a marker for analysis of  disease progression  i believe that integrating uh  other omics big data could give us  better results and find important  prognostic markers but the expression  library used also seems to  be a bit lacking  as we saw in the mechanism of pd1 action  inclusion of other cell types like  helper t cells and regulatory t cells  could possibly fill this gap  and  after some discussions the time duration  between measurement of response could be  one of the factors causing less  differential gene expression  like uh six to eight weeks between  response response measurement according  to resist guidelines apply for tumor  cells  the change in gene expression of t cells  precedes the tumor reduction that it  would mediate  so a few weeks before this time or  increasing the frequency of sample  extraction could improve the study  greatly  and  using different algorithms and  analytical tools is always an option  but  the probability of that alone improving  our results is very slim  so in conclusion  dealing with lung cancer is still not in  a stage where we can confidently look  for cures  the clinicians are currently focused on  controlling growth of tumor and a  palliative care  and immune checkpoint inhibitors show  promise on paper and also in many trials  but to make up for the dearth of  response use of combination therapies is  growing more popular and  the second regimen in the combination  therapy being  of chemotherapeutic agent like cisplatin  or other immune checkpoint blockers like  atazolizumab  or epillumab  so  the till these trials give us adequate  results tobacco eradication must be our  number one step against lung cancer that  is it for my report and i'd like to take  some time to thank my mentors and guides  dr raghav and sonalika mam for helping  me put this together and working through  this project thank you so much

